\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. What Middleware Is\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Middleware gives 
\f0\b tighter control over what happens inside an agent
\f1\b0 , without adding new capability to the agent itself.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Without middleware, the flow is simply: 
\f0\b Request 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Model call 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Tool execute 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Response
\f1\b0 . Middleware lets you hook into this flow at defined points.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Analogy: guardrails on a highway/mountain road \'97 they don't change the destination, but they keep the vehicle (agent) from going off track.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Same concept exists in every agent framework under different names: 
\f0\b Middleware
\f1\b0  (LangChain), 
\f0\b Callbacks
\f1\b0  (Google ADK), 
\f0\b Hooks
\f1\b0  (Claude Code).\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Middleware is used for: logging/analytics/debugging, transforming prompts or output formatting, adding retries/fallbacks/early termination, rate limiting, guardrails, and PII detection.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Guardrails vs Middleware:
\f1\b0  Guardrail is the 
\f2\i concept
\f1\i0  (controlling agent behavior so it stays safe/compliant); Middleware is 
\f2\i one way
\f1\i0  of implementing guardrails (system-prompt instructions are another way).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Real-world/industry examples given: fraud detection in fintech (wrap tool call around every transaction), HIPAA-driven PII middleware in healthcare bots, human-in-the-loop in customer support (cancel/refund flows), audit logging in internal developer tools.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Execution points ("hooks") in the agent lifecycle\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs26 \cf0 Request 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [before agent] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  agent 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [before model] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [wrap model call] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  model\
        
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [after model] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [wrap tool call] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  tool execute 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  [after agent] 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  Response\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain does 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  provide explicit "before tool"/"after tool" hooks \'97 that's handled via 
\f0\b wrap tool call
\f1\b0  instead (a framework-specific choice; other frameworks like ADK do expose before/after tool).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 before agent
\f1\fs24  / 
\f3\fs26 after agent
\f1\fs24  run 
\f0\b once per invocation
\f1\b0  (good for opening/closing DB connections, setup/teardown).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 before model
\f1\fs24  / 
\f3\fs26 wrap model call
\f1\fs24  / 
\f3\fs26 after model
\f1\fs24  can run 
\f0\b multiple times
\f1\b0  per invocation (once per model call in the agentic loop).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Pre-Built (Inbuilt) Middlewares in LangChain\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 2.1 Summarization Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Automatically summarizes conversation history as it approaches token limits (like ChatGPT/Claude's "compact" feature; Claude Code's 
\f3\fs26 /compact
\f1\fs24 ).\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 model
\f1\fs24  (can differ from the main agent's brain \'97 e.g., a cheaper model just for summarizing), 
\f3\fs26 trigger
\f1\fs24  (token count / message count / fraction of context length), 
\f3\fs26 keep
\f1\fs24  (how much recent context to preserve \'97 tokens, messages, or fraction).\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Trade-off: summarizing risks losing information \'97 no way to guarantee 100% retention. Mitigate with a long-term memory/store (in-memory, PostgreSQL, etc.) alongside summarization.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When triggered, LangChain replaces old messages with a synthetic 
\f0\b human message
\f1\b0  containing a structured summary (session intent, artifacts, next steps).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.2 Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pauses agent execution before a sensitive tool call for human approval/edit/reject, similar to Claude asking permission before an action.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Use cases: high-stakes ops (DB writes, financial transactions), compliance workflows, long-running conversations needing steering.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 interrupt_on
\f1\fs24  = mapping of tool name 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  allowed decisions.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Four built-in human decisions:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Approve
\f1\b0  \'97 execute tool with original arguments\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Edit
\f1\b0  \'97 modify the tool arguments before executing\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reject
\f1\b0  \'97 skip execution, return rejection feedback to the agent\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Respond
\f1\b0  \'97 human's text becomes a synthetic tool/agent message (for "ask user" style tools), skipping execution\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Requires a 
\f0\b checkpointer
\f1\b0  (e.g., 
\f3\fs26 InMemorySaver
\f1\fs24 ) + 
\f3\fs26 thread_id
\f1\fs24  in config, because the flow is paused mid-execution and must resume with full context.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Resuming uses LangGraph's 
\f3\fs26 Command(resume=\{...decision...\})
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 HITL is 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  the same as "transfer to a human agent" (that's a hand-off, not an approval gate) \'97 HITL means the 
\f2\i agent itself
\f1\i0  asks for approval before an action.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 HITL is framework-agnostic \'97 present in ADK, LangGraph, every agent framework.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.3 Model Call Limit Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Prevents runaway loops / excessive cost by capping how many times the model (brain) can be called.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Two knobs: 
\f0\b run limit
\f1\b0  (max calls within a single 
\f3\fs26 .invoke()
\f1\fs24 ) and 
\f0\b thread limit
\f1\b0  (max calls across the whole conversation/thread \'97 requires checkpointer).\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Analogy: run limit = max chapatis per meal; thread limit = max chapatis per day.\
