\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.4 Tool Call Limit Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Same idea as model call limit but for 
\f0\b tool calls
\f1\b0  \'97 globally across all tools, or per specific tool.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Prevents: excessive calls to expensive external APIs, unlimited web searches/DB queries, runaway agent loops.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Also has 
\f0\b thread limit
\f1\b0  (across whole conversation) and 
\f0\b run limit
\f1\b0  (per single invoke) \'97 reset with each new user message.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 exit_behavior
\f1\fs24  options: 
\f3\fs26 continue
\f1\fs24  (default \'97 return an error tool message, let agent/model keep going), 
\f3\fs26 error
\f1\fs24  (raise 
\f3\fs26 ToolCallLimitExceededError
\f1\fs24  and stop immediately), or a custom callable.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Example: 
\f3\fs26 cancel_booking
\f1\fs24  limited to 1x per run / 2x per thread, while all tools together capped at 8 globally.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Demonstrated that when a limit is hit, the tool returns an 
\f0\b error tool message
\f1\b0  (not an interrupt) \'97 execution continues, but the AI reports it can't perform the action again.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.5 Model Fallback Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If the primary model fails (outage, invalid key, 404, etc.), automatically falls back to a secondary model (e.g., GPT-5.5 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  GPT-5.4-mini).\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 first model
\f1\fs24  + 
\f3\fs26 additional model(s)
\f1\fs24  \'97 any number of fallbacks, though >5 is generally excessive.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Distinguished from "routing": fallback only triggers on failure, not based on task type or speed.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rationale: production apps can't just say "sorry, service unavailable" during a provider outage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

