\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.7 To-Do List Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Equips the agent with task planning/tracking for complex, multi-step tasks (mirrors what Claude/Claude Code/Claude Co-work show as a visible checklist/plan).\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Generates a structured to-do list (task descriptions + status: pending/in-progress/completed) that updates as the agent works through steps sequentially.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 More reliable than just instructing "make a plan" via system prompt \'97 this middleware creates an actual structured to-do 
\f2\i object
\f1\i0 , not just a text plan.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.8 LLM Tool Selector Middleware ("dynamic tool loading" by query)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Uses a (cheaper) LLM to pick only the tools relevant to the current user query 
\f0\b before
\f1\b0  the main model is called \'97 reduces token usage and improves accuracy/focus.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Distinct from earlier "dynamic tool loading" based on user state/flags (e.g., pro vs free user) \'97 this one filters based on the 
\f0\b content of the query itself
\f1\b0 .\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 model
\f1\fs24  (selector model, can be cheaper than main agent model), 
\f3\fs26 max_tools
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 always_include
\f1\fs24  (tools that are always sent and don't count against the max).\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mirrors how Claude/Claude.ai loads only relevant connector tools instead of sending every connected tool on every call.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rule of thumb: if you can determine tool relevance with plain code logic (e.g., "if user not logged in, remove book_seats"), do that instead of relying on an LLM \'97 code is deterministic, LLMs are fuzzy and can err.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.9 Tool Error Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Catches exceptions raised during tool execution and converts them into a 
\f0\b tool error message
\f1\b0  the model can see and self-correct from, instead of crashing the whole agent.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 on_error
\f1\fs24  (a function returning a friendlier/sanitized error message), optionally scoped to specific 
\f3\fs26 tools
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Does 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  automatically retry \'97 that's Tool Retry Middleware's job.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Real effect: LLM sees the error and can decide to retry with corrected arguments (its own judgment), or apologize/report failure gracefully instead of throwing a raw stack trace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.10 Tool Retry Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Automatically retries a failed tool call with 
\f0\b exponential backoff
\f1\b0 .\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Config: 
\f3\fs26 max_retries
\f1\fs24  (retries 
\f2\i after
\f1\i0  the initial call, so 
\f3\fs26 max_retries=3
\f1\fs24  
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  up to 4 total calls), 
\f3\fs26 initial_delay
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 max_delay
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 backoff_factor
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 jitter
\f1\fs24  (randomization to avoid "thundering herd"), 
\f3\fs26 retry_on
\f1\fs24  (specific exception types), 
\f3\fs26 on_failure
\f1\fs24  (
\f3\fs26 continue
\f1\fs24  
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  return error tool message; 
\f3\fs26 error
\f1\fs24  
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  raise).\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Delay formula: 
\f3\fs26 initial_delay \'d7 backoff_factor^retry_number
\f1\fs24 .\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Demonstrated with a random-failure tool: 4 calls (1 initial + 3 retries) failed 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  returned an error tool message 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  the LLM itself decided to retry the 
\f2\i tool call
\f1\i0  again (another 4 attempts) because it looked like a transient issue \'97 total 8 calls observed. This illustrates that 
\f0\b the LLM's own retry decision is separate from and on top of
\f1\b0  the middleware's built-in retry count, and can be constrained via system prompt instructions if undesired.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.11 LLM Tool Emulator Middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For development/testing: instead of actually calling a real tool (e.g., a payment tool, or one that's expensive/unfinished), an LLM 
