\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.2 Hooks \'97 the extension points\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Definition (from LangChain docs, emphasized as important):
\f1\b0  
\f2\i "Hooks are extension points in custom middleware that let you intercept, inspect, or modify agent execution at specific stages of the lifecycle."
\f1\i0 \
Two styles of hooks:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Node-style hooks
\f1\b0  \'97 run sequentially at a specific execution point: 
\f3\fs26 before_agent
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 before_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 after_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 after_agent
\f1\fs24 . Used for logging, validation, state updates. You get access to 
\f0\b state
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b runtime
\f1\b0 .\
\ls17\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wrap-style hooks
\f1\b0  \'97 wrap a call entirely: 
\f3\fs26 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 wrap_tool_call
\f1\fs24 . You get access to the 
\f0\b exact request object
\f1\b0  being sent (and must return a response), so you can 
\f2\i modify
\f1\i0  it in-flight (e.g., swap the model, strip PII, filter tools) rather than just observe it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Practical distinction demonstrated:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 before_model
\f1\fs24  (node-style): good for logging/read-only awareness right before a model call.\
\ls18\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24  (wrap-style): used for 
\f0\b dynamic model selection
\f1\b0  \'97 e.g., swap in a cheaper/basic model vs. an advanced model based on 
\f3\fs26 len(messages) > 10
\f1\fs24 , directly mutating the outgoing model request. This is conceptually the same mechanism behind ChatGPT/Claude's "auto model selection."\
\ls18\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 wrap_model_call
\f1\fs24  was also used earlier (in the tools lecture) for 
\f0\b dynamic tool filtering
\f1\b0  \'97 removing tools (e.g., "VIP-only" booking tool) from the outgoing request based on a condition, before the model ever sees them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.3 Agent state & runtime (brief LangGraph preview)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0
\f3\b0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 create_agent
\f1\fs24  runs on a 
\f0\b LangGraph runtime
\f1\b0  under the hood, which maintains: static info, user ID, connections/dependencies, context, store, execution info, etc.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The agent also has a 
\f0\b state
\f1\b0  object (e.g., a custom field like 
\f3\fs26 is_vip_member
\f1\fs24  set earlier in the course) that middleware can read and 
\f0\b update
\f1\b0 .\
\ls19\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Key point: 
\f0\b middleware can read and mutate agent state/runtime
\f1\b0  \'97 that's 
\f2\i how
\f1\i0  it achieves its effect (e.g., PII middleware changing what's visible in state before it reaches the model).\
\ls19\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Full depth of state/runtime will be covered properly in the upcoming LangGraph module.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.4 Two ways to define custom middleware\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Decorator-based
\f1\b0  \'97 quick, simple, single-hook middleware. E.g.: 
\f3\fs26 @before_modeldef log_before_model(state, runtime):    print("About to call model with these messages so far...")\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\fs24 Good for: single hook needed, no complex config, quick prototyping.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Class-based
\f1\b0  \'97 subclass 
\f3\fs26 AgentMiddleware
\f1\fs24 ; can define multiple hooks (
\f3\fs26 before_model
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 after_model
\f1\fs24 , etc.) as methods, both sync and async versions, plus custom 
\f3\fs26 state_schema
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 tools
\f1\fs24 , and stream 
\f3\fs26 transformers
\f1\fs24  as class attributes picked up by the agent factory at compile time. Good for: multiple hooks in one middleware, sync+async needed, complex/reusable configuration, production use.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Example custom middleware live-coded:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 connect_to_db
\f1\fs24  (before_agent) / 
\f3\fs26 disconnect_from_db
\f1\fs24  (after_agent) \'97 setup/teardown that should only run once per invocation.\
\ls21\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 CallCounterMiddleware
\f1\fs24  (class-based) \'97 increments a call counter in 
\f3\fs26 before_model
\f1\fs24  and prints a warning ("keep your credit card ready") once a threshold is crossed \'97 illustrates that one middleware class can hold custom internal state.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 3.5 Multiple middlewares & execution order\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A single middleware can register 
\f0\b multiple hook points
\f1\b0  (analogy: "tasting food" middleware \'97 hooked both 
\f2\i after adding salt
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i before serving to guests
\f1\i0 ; same middleware, two hook points).\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 With multiple middlewares in a list 
